1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,048
How do you make sure a card is charged exactly once?

2
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:09,552
The buyer clicks pay twice, or the page retries, or two copies of the app handle the same confirmation.

3
00:00:09,602 --> 00:00:11,328
Each of them would charge the card.

4
00:00:11,378 --> 00:00:17,376
And if the app crashes after asking the network, but before saving the answer, nobody knows whether money moved.

5
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:20,016
A bank's cash desk solves both problems.

6
00:00:20,066 --> 00:00:22,872
Only one cashier can hold the withdrawal slip.

7
00:00:22,922 --> 00:00:26,856
And the cashier writes it in the logbook before opening the drawer.

8
00:00:26,906 --> 00:00:28,728
The debit works the same way.

9
00:00:28,778 --> 00:00:34,176
First comes the claim: one conditional update moves the payment from card confirmed to debiting.

10
00:00:34,226 --> 00:00:38,904
Only one caller's update can match, so exactly one caller wins.

11
00:00:38,954 --> 00:00:41,856
The winner finds the live terminal and the bank's fee.

12
00:00:41,906 --> 00:00:45,600
Without either, the payment fails, and nothing is charged.

13
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:49,872
Then it writes a row in the operation journal, before the network is asked.

14
00:00:49,922 --> 00:00:54,312
The journal holds one debit per payment, and the database enforces it.

15
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:57,456
ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token.

16
00:00:57,506 --> 00:01:02,136
The call carries an id that makes it happen once, even if it is sent twice.

17
00:01:02,186 --> 00:01:05,088
Some card networks only hold the money at first.

18
00:01:05,138 --> 00:01:09,168
For those, the card zone takes it at once, and that is a capture.

19
00:01:09,218 --> 00:01:12,960
Approved means paid, with the network's reference number.

20
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:14,832
Declined is final.

21
00:01:14,882 --> 00:01:20,856
And no answer in time is unknown, never declined: Lesson 27 picks it up.

22
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:22,728
Now read it in the code.

23
00:01:22,778 --> 00:01:27,960
The claim is a single update: set debiting, where the payment is still card confirmed.

24
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:32,808
Of any number of callers, exactly one changes the row, and gets a one back.

25
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:38,064
The journal table is written before every call to a network, and finished with the answer.

26
00:01:38,114 --> 00:01:42,504
And one unique constraint allows just one debit for each payment.

27
00:01:42,554 --> 00:01:47,688
Like the cash desk: one slip, written in the logbook first, and one charge.

28
00:01:47,738 --> 00:01:50,784
Next: what happens when the network goes silent.
